Random Variable
\[
X_1 =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if a randomly selected student using the study app passes} \\
0 & \text{if a randomly selected student using the study app does not pass}
\end{cases}
\]
\[
X_2 =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if a randomly selected student not using the study app passes} \\
0 & \text{if a randomly selected student not using the study app does not pass}
\end{cases}
\]
Are these normal random variables?

Standard Error
\[
SE_{\hat{p}_1-\hat{p}_2}
=
\sqrt{
\hat{p}_{\text{pooled}}
(1-\hat{p}_{\text{pooled}})
\left(
\frac{1}{n_1}+\frac{1}{n_2}
\right)
}
\]
\[
SE_{\hat{p}_1-\hat{p}_2}
=
\sqrt{
(0.76)(0.24)
\left(
\frac{1}{50}+\frac{1}{50}
\right)
}
\]
\[
SE_{\hat{p}_1-\hat{p}_2}
=
\sqrt{0.007296}
\approx 0.0854
\]

z-statistic
\[
z =
\frac{\text{observed statistic} - \text{null value}}
{\text{standard error}}
\] … and we know these things!

z-statistic
\[
z =
\frac{
\hat{p}_1-\hat{p}_2 - (p_{1o} - p_{2o})
}{
\sqrt{
\hat{p}_{\text{pooled}}
(1-\hat{p}_{\text{pooled}})
\left(
\frac{1}{n_1}+\frac{1}{n_2}
\right)
}
}
\]

z-score
\[
z = \frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}
= \frac{9-5}{1.5}
= \frac{4}{1.5}
\approx 2.67
\]
So, a 9-minute wait is approximately 2.67 standard deviations above the mean.
And this is unusual! Anything above 2 SDs above the mean is considered unlikely. Think back to the emperical rule :).

